R = [record('mudd-android-serviced-captivity', 'c0382', 'Star Trek — Mudd’s android-serviced captivity', 'Star Trek: The Original Series', 'Harry Mudd’s existing supported residence on the android planet', ['tv'],
    scope('TOS I, Mudd', 'Mudd’s established residence before the Enterprise escape and android reprogramming', 'Harry Mudd, the resident living human receiving android service', ['Temporary Enterprise captives as a stable resident population', 'The unimplemented plan to control humanity', 'The original extinct makers', 'Post-reprogramming parole as an identical political system']),
    'Mudd already lives among androids that supply personal services and productive support after their makers have died. The retained unit is his narrow existing residence, not the machines’ proposed expansion across human civilization. His support does not depend on a wage or an outside human household staff. That gives the arrangement a strong material-guarantee estimate within its tiny scope. Captivity is a major restriction on freedom, but refusal to let him leave does not itself prove that food or care is withheld.\n\nMudd presents himself as a ruler, yet the androids can reject his commands and prevent his departure. Their coordinated machine authority therefore matters more than his imperial title when estimating effective control. This point lies toward autonomous AI provision, with uncertainty about the exact mixture of accepted requests and binding restrictions. Personal enjoyment, manipulation and confinement do not provide a trustworthy work census, so the participation axis remains unresolved. The crew’s later reprogramming changes the arrangement and is not silently treated as the same authority structure.',
    {'guarantees': -0.65, 'control': -0.8, 'nonworking': None},
    {'guarantees': assessment(-0.65, [-0.9, -0.25], 'low', 'Existing android service supports the sole human resident without a wage; confinement is distinguished from demonstrated withdrawal of material care.', m),
     'control': assessment(-0.8, [-1, -0.3], 'medium', 'The android network overrides Mudd’s wishes, including departure, despite his apparent command over many personal services.', m),
     'nonworking': assessment(None, None, 'low', 'The resident’s activities are not a stable all-purpose labor census; no exact leisure fraction is supplied.', m)},
    5, m, [evidence('Mudd already receives android service, but the machines refuse to permit his departure.', m, 'Episode/reference pre-escape residence and android agenda', 'secondary_episode_reference_and_criticism')],
    ['A single-human continuing support unit, explicitly limited in size.', 'Mudd’s title is not effective productive ownership.', 'Only the pre-reprogramming authority is scored.', 'Promises to serve humanity at large are not an enacted economy.']),
    record('aldea-custodian-provision', 'c0386', 'Star Trek — Aldea under the Custodian', 'Star Trek: The Next Generation', 'Aldean residents supported by the Custodian', ['tv'],
    scope('TNG When the Bough Breaks', 'Established Custodian provision before the defense-system shutdown and technical relearning', 'Living biological Aldean residents, used as a humanoid-person comparison proxy', ['Kidnapped Enterprise children as a stable separate society', 'Post-intervention recovery as already identical', 'Computers as additional human citizens'], proxy=True),
    'Aldea has a direct technological support system: the Custodian supplies residents’ needs while recommending pursuits such as the arts. Ordinary provision is therefore materially separated from necessary production jobs. The guarantee estimate is strong for residents, but it does not imply perfect health protection. The planetary defenses have damaging environmental effects, and the population has lost the knowledge needed to diagnose the resulting infertility. The scope is the existing Aldean community before its systems are changed. Biological humanoid residents are an explicit comparison proxy for the human-centered axes.\n\nResidents can command the Custodian through personal interfaces, and its access clearances show structured humanlike participation rather than an unquestionable AI sovereign. The broad positive control estimate reflects dispersed practical access, while leaving uncertainty about elite privileges and the ability to alter underlying infrastructure. Lack of engineering knowledge is a vulnerability, not sufficient proof that the machine owns everything. Artistic study and other chosen activity can still be useful work, so automatic provision does not justify a numerical nonworking share. Later technical relearning is not projected backward into this period.',
    {'guarantees': -0.8, 'control': 0.5, 'nonworking': None},
    {'guarantees': assessment(-0.8, [-1, -0.4], 'medium', 'Resident needs are supplied by a continuing system, but environmentally induced infertility and lost maintenance knowledge qualify its health/security floor.', a),
     'control': assessment(0.5, [0.1, 0.8], 'low', 'Residents have command interfaces and clearances, but the reference does not fully establish collective ownership or the distribution of privileged access.', a),
     'nonworking': assessment(None, None, 'low', 'Recommended artistic study and chosen useful pursuits do not yield an all-person participation census.', a)},
    2, a, [evidence('The Custodian provides needs, accepts resident commands and recommends studies while the defense system damages the environment.', a, 'Custodian description and episode-linked aftermath', 'secondary_tv_lore_reference')],
    ['Explicit biological humanoid-person proxy, not an undocumented human population.', 'Fan reference evidence remains provisional and is one chain rather than independent corroboration.', 'Control is broad and uncertain; resident command is not automatically common legal ownership.', 'Kidnapping and the later intervention are not additional settled societies.'], alternatives=[1])]
